==English==

===Etymology===
From Late {{inh|en|enm|incessaunte}}, from {{der|en|LL.|incessāns|incessāns, incessantem}}, from {{der|en|la|-}} {{af|la|in-|cessāns|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈsɛs.ənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-incessant.wav|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-incessant.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|in|ces|sant}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛsənt|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Without [[pause]] or [[stop]]; not [[ending]], especially to the point of annoyance.
#: {{syn|en|unremitting|continuous|unceasing}}
#: {{ux|en|The dog's '''incessant''' barking kept the girl awake all night.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1807|author=George Campbell|title=Lectures on Ecclesiastical History|page=274
|passage={{...}} '''incessant''' interferings and bickerings, in every country, between the secular powers and the ecclesiastical.}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|author=Charles Darwin|title=w:On the Origin of Species
|year=1859
|passage=The face of Nature may be compared to a yielding surface, with ten thousand sharp wedges packed close together and driven inwards by '''incessant''' blows, sometimes one wedge being struck, and then another with greater force.}}
#* {{seeMoreCites|en}}

